artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the following ten-parameter SAIR/V+S compartmental epidemic model with standard (frequency-dependent) incidence. The population is divided into four compartments: susceptibles S(t), exposed/asymptomatic infectives E(t), symptomatic infectives I(t), and recovered/vaccinated R(t), with total alive population N(t)=S(t)+E(t)+I(t)+R(t). The dynamics are governed by

  S' = Λ − (β_E E + β_I I) S / N − (μ + γ_v) S + γ_r R,
  E' = (β_E E + β_I I) S / N − (μ + ε + δ_E) E,
  I' = ε E − (μ + γ + δ_I) I,
  R' = γ I + γ_v S − (μ + γ_r) R,

where the ten nonnegative parameters are:
 • Λ > 0, a constant (linear) birth/recruitment rate;
 • μ > 0, the natural death rate;
 • β_E ≥ 0 and β_I ≥ 0, the transmission rates from the exposed/asymptomatic and infective classes;
 • ε > 0, the rate at which exposed/asymptomatic individuals become symptomatic infectives;
 • γ > 0, the recovery rate of infectives;
 • δ_E ≥ 0 and δ_I ≥ 0, the disease-induced death rates in the E and I compartments;
 • γ_v ≥ 0, the vaccination rate of susceptibles;
 • γ_r ≥ 0, the rate of loss of immunity.

Let R₀ denote the basic reproduction number of this system, computed via the next-generation matrix at the disease-free equilibrium. It is known that a unique disease-free equilibrium exists, and that a unique endemic equilibrium exists when R₀ > 1, with local asymptotic stability of the disease-free equilibrium when R₀ < 1 and of the endemic equilibrium when R₀ > 1.

Prove or disprove: for all admissible values of the ten parameters,
 (a) when R₀ ≤ 1, the disease-free equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable in the biologically relevant invariant region;
 (b) when R₀ > 1, the unique endemic equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable in the interior of the biologically relevant invariant region.

